WEBVTT

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:03.788
Not all tokens should be treated equally.

00:00:03.788 --> 00:00:08.381
This is HiCCR, Hierarchical Context Credit Reassignment,

00:00:08.381 --> 00:00:14.450
our NeurIPS 2026 paper on reinforcement learning for multimodal reasoning.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.127
When a vision-language model is trained with verifiable rewards,

00:00:20.127 --> 00:00:25.585
algorithms like GRPO and DAPO hand every token in a response the same advantage.

00:00:25.585 --> 00:00:31.360
The token that reads a clock hand from the image gets exactly the same credit as the word "therefore".

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.168
Our first finding: uniform credit is suboptimal.

00:00:36.168 --> 00:00:42.512
Simply multiplying each token's advantage by random noise already beats uniform credit,

00:00:42.512 --> 00:00:44.043
in all four settings.

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:50.993
For DAPO on the 8B model, accuracy rises from 68.3 to 71.4.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:58.198
Second finding: under uniform credit, RL tuning weakens visual grounding.

00:00:58.198 --> 00:01:03.527
Along three axes, attention to the image, gradient attribution to image tokens,

00:01:03.527 --> 00:01:07.305
and the accuracy lost when image embeddings are removed,

00:01:07.305 --> 00:01:10.273
the tuned model relies less on what it sees.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.718
Why does this happen?

00:01:12.718 --> 00:01:15.587
Pretrained models carry a language prior:

00:01:15.587 --> 00:01:19.855
text context produces larger activations than visual context.

00:01:19.855 --> 00:01:25.700
Under this imbalance, a uniform positive advantage sends more gradient to the text side,

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.892
and visual attention drifts down.

00:01:27.892 --> 00:01:33.168
Uniform credit amplifies a bias that pretraining already put in place.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.655
HiCCR starts from a single signal: context credit.

00:01:38.655 --> 00:01:44.590
For each generated token, we measure how concentrated its attention is within the image,

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.343
using the last two layers.

00:01:46.343 --> 00:01:48.830
Focused attention means high credit.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.168
Diffuse attention means low credit.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.405
Credit is then reassigned at two levels.

00:01:55.405 --> 00:01:59.843
At the token level, grounded tokens get a larger share of the advantage.

00:01:59.843 --> 00:02:04.768
At the trajectory level, rollouts that engage more with the image are upweighted.

00:02:04.768 --> 00:02:07.196
And only correct rollouts are reshaped,

00:02:07.196 --> 00:02:11.305
so a wrong answer is never punished just for looking at the image.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:18.486
Across nine reasoning benchmarks, HiCCR improves its base algorithm in every setting,

00:02:18.486 --> 00:02:21.108
by up to eight points on the 4B model.

00:02:21.108 --> 00:02:31.340
With only eight billion parameters, HiCCR-D reaches 72.8, ahead of Qwen2.5-VL-72B,

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.958
Gemini 2.0 Flash, and GPT-4o.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:42.110
It adds under three percent training time, with no auxiliary model and no extra data.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:46.724
And the gains carry over to InternVL3, to geometry-only training,

00:02:46.724 --> 00:02:50.060
and to perception and hallucination benchmarks.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.702
Without any token-level supervision,

00:02:53.702 --> 00:02:59.248
the highest credit lands on tokens that refer to visual content: numbers on the clock,

00:02:59.248 --> 00:03:00.280
objects, shapes.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:04.230
And the erosion of visual grounding is largely reversed.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.390
Not all tokens are equal.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:10.365
Give credit where the image is actually used.

00:03:10.365 --> 00:03:11.903
That's HiCCR.
